/* data/learn-c/phy-12-15.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-15.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-15"] = {"15.1":{"s":"The idea that matter is made of atoms is very old, from the Greek thinkers to Dalton. This chapter follows how experiments slowly showed what is inside an atom. First came electrons, then a tiny nucleus and fixed energy levels. Last came the nucleus itself and radioactivity."},"15.2":{"s":"After discovering the electron, Thomson pictured the atom as a sphere of spread-out positive charge with electrons stuck in it, like seeds in a watermelon. The model kept the atom neutral, but it could not explain the scattering experiment that followed."},"15.3":{"s":"Geiger and Marsden fired alpha particles at a very thin gold foil. Almost all went straight through. A few were deflected through large angles, and a very small number bounced almost straight back. Thomson's spread-out charge could never turn an alpha particle back.","w":["Most alpha particles going straight through shows the atom is mostly empty space."]},"15.4":{"s":"Rutherford concluded that all the positive charge and almost all the mass sit in a tiny central nucleus, with electrons orbiting far outside. The atom is mostly empty space. But an orbiting electron should radiate energy and spiral into the nucleus, so the model could not explain why atoms are stable.","w":["Rutherford's model fails on two counts: atoms are stable, and they give line (not continuous) spectra."]},"15.5":{"s":"Hot hydrogen gas does not give a continuous rainbow. It gives sharp lines at fixed wavelengths: a line spectrum. The lines fall into series (Lyman, Balmer, Paschen and others), and their wavelengths follow a simple formula with the Rydberg constant.","f":[{"x":"1/lambda = R*(1/n^2 - 1/m^2)","n":"Lines of hydrogen; m > n. n = 1 Lyman (UV), 2 Balmer (visible), 3 Paschen (IR), then Brackett, Pfund.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.5, p.326"}],"w":["The series limit is the shortest wavelength of a series (m → ∞): λ = n²/R."]},"15.6":{"s":"Bohr's postulates: electrons move only in certain orbits, without radiating. In these orbits angular momentum is nh/2π. Light is emitted or absorbed only when an electron jumps between orbits, with hν equal to the energy difference. For hydrogen, orbit radius grows as n² and energy is −13.6/n² eV. This explains the spectral series.","f":[{"x":"m_e*v_n*r_n = n*h/(2*pi)","n":"Bohr's second postulate: angular momentum is quantised.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.6, p.327"},{"x":"r_n = a_0*n^2","n":"Orbit radius in hydrogen; Bohr radius a_0 = 0.053 nm.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.6, p.327"},{"x":"E_n = -13.6*Z^2/n^2 eV","n":"Energy of the electron in the n-th orbit (Z = 1 for hydrogen).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.6, p.328"},{"x":"1/lambda = R_H*Z^2*(1/n^2 - 1/m^2)","n":"Rydberg's formula from Bohr's model; R_H = 1.097 × 10^7 per metre.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.6, p.329"},{"x":"2*pi*r_n = n*lambda_n","n":"De Broglie: a whole number of electron waves fits round each allowed orbit.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.6, p.330"}],"w":["Energy is negative: the electron is bound. Ionisation energy of hydrogen = +13.6 eV.","First excitation energy of hydrogen: −3.4 − (−13.6) = 10.2 eV."]},"15.7":{"s":"The nucleus is made of protons and neutrons, together called nucleons. Z is the number of protons (atomic number) and A is the number of nucleons (mass number). Isotopes have the same Z but different A. Nuclear radius grows as A^(1/3), so all nuclei have about the same density. A strong, short-range nuclear force holds nucleons together.","f":[{"x":"A = Z + N","n":"Mass number = protons + neutrons.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.7, p.331"},{"x":"1 u = 931.5 MeV/c^2","n":"Atomic mass unit (1/12 of a carbon-12 atom) as energy.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.7, p.331"},{"x":"R = R_0*A^(1/3)","n":"Nuclear radius; R_0 = 1.2 × 10^-15 m.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.7, p.332"}],"w":["Isotopes: same Z. Isobars: same A. Isotones: same N."]},"15.8":{"s":"A nucleus has less mass than its separate protons and neutrons. The missing mass (mass defect) has become binding energy: E = Δm c², with 1 u equal to about 931.5 MeV. Binding energy per nucleon is highest near iron, so splitting very heavy nuclei or joining very light ones releases energy.","f":[{"x":"Delta M = Z*m_p + N*m_n - M","n":"Mass defect of a nucleus of mass M.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.8, p.333"},{"x":"E_B = (Z*m_H + N*m_n - M)*c^2","n":"Binding energy using atomic masses: m_H = mass of a hydrogen atom, M = mass of the whole atom (not the bare nucleus).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.8, p.333"}],"w":["Compare stability by binding energy PER NUCLEON, not total binding energy."]},"15.9":{"s":"Unstable nuclei decay. Alpha decay emits a helium nucleus (A falls by 4, Z by 2). Beta-minus decay turns a neutron into a proton and emits an electron and an antineutrino (Z rises by 1). Gamma decay emits a high-energy photon and changes neither A nor Z.","f":[{"x":"Q = (m_X - m_Y - m_He)*c^2","n":"Energy released in alpha decay (atomic masses).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.9, p.334"}],"w":["Beta-minus: a neutron becomes a proton, so A stays the same and Z rises by 1."]},"15.10":{"s":"Decay is random, but the rate is proportional to the number of nuclei present: N = N₀ e^(−λt). The half-life, T½ = 0.693/λ, is the time for half the nuclei to decay. Mean life is 1/λ. Activity, the decays per second, is measured in becquerel; 1 curie is 3.7 × 10¹⁰ Bq.","f":[{"x":"N = N_0*e^(-lambda*t)","n":"Law of radioactive decay; lambda = decay constant.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.10, p.336"},{"x":"A = lambda*N = A_0*e^(-lambda*t)","n":"Activity (decays per second, becquerel).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.10, p.336"},{"x":"T_(1/2) = 0.693/lambda","n":"Half-life.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.10, p.337"},{"x":"tau = 1/lambda","n":"Average (mean) life; T_(1/2) = 0.693 tau.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.10, p.337"}],"w":["After n half-lives the fraction left is (1/2)ⁿ; it never reaches exactly zero."]},"15.11":{"s":"Nuclear energy comes from mass turning into energy. In fission, a heavy nucleus such as U-235 absorbs a neutron and splits, releasing energy and more neutrons. That allows a chain reaction, which is controlled in a reactor. In fusion, light nuclei join at extremely high temperature. Fusion powers the Sun."}};
